#logs 

#logs help with monitoring and troubleshooting errors, but they consume disk space

#logrotate application keeps logs in check. This allows us to specify how many days of logs
#to keep. This allows us to split logs into manageable files, compresss them to save space,
#or keep them in a separate server.

#log control can be automated

#looking in logs dir /var/log, and subdirectories like /var/log/apache2, you can see that
#your logs are already being rotated. There should be a /var/log/syslog file, but also a 
#series of older compressed versions with names like /var/log/syslog.1.gz

#remembering that cron runs scripts in /etc/cron.daily - looking in there should reveal a 
#script called logrotate - or possibly 00logrotate to force it to be the first task to run

#the overall configuration of logrotate is set in /etc/logrotate.conf; have a look at that,
# but also look at the files in the directory /etc/logrotate.d, as the contents there 
#are merged in to create the full configuration. You will probably see one called apache2,
#with contents like

 /var/log/apache2/*.log {
 weekly
 missingok
 rotate 52
 compress
 delaycompress
 notifempty
 create 640 root adm
 }

#any apache2.log file will be rotated each week, while 52 compressed copies will be kept

#typicaaly installing an application will come with a suitable logrotate recipe installed
#as well. You normally don't need to create these from scratch. 
# However, default settings don't always match requirements, so it is reasonable for you
#as an sysadmin to edit these. #for example, instead of apache2's default behavior of
# creating 52 weekly logs, you might want logs created daily and mailed to an auditor, and
#only keep 30 days worth if logs on the server.


 
